From c57ef20faa29c71df4284b860eaeb48f98806c8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 300/488] soh: name the 8 address-named symbols defined in
 z_bgcheck.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 8 are defined in soh/src/code/z_bgcheck.c; updating their references touches 11 files in total.

  * func_80038A28 -> CollisionPoly_GetTransformMtx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: its doc comment reads "Compute transform
    matrix mapping +y (up) to the collision poly's normal"; callers use it
    to stand actors and effects upright on a slope.

  * func_800418D0 -> BgCheck_ResetDynaBgHeaders
    deduced, confidence GUESS: its doc comment reads "SEGMENT_TO_VIRTUAL all
    active BgActor CollisionHeaders"; it re-resolves every live bg actor's
    collision header after the object segments move, which is why the pause
    menu and the save menu call it on close. "Reset" names that re-resolve
    pass.

  * func_80041D70 -> SurfaceType_GetUnkProperty
    deduced, confidence GUESS: its doc comment reads "SurfaceType Get ?
    Property (& 0x001C 0000)"; it extracts bits 18 to 20 of the surface type
    word, and the question mark in that comment is the whole of what is
    known about the field. Nothing calls it.

  * func_80042708 -> CollisionPoly_GetPointOnIntersectLine
    deduced, confidence HIGH: its doc comment reads "Gets the closestPoint
    to point on the line formed from the intersection of planes polyA and
    polyB"; it is a thin wrapper over Math3D_PlaneVsPlaneVsLineClosestPoint.

  * func_800427B4 -> CollisionPoly_GetPointOnLineSeg
    deduced, confidence HIGH: its doc comment reads "Get the closestPoint to
    line (pointA, pointB) formed from the intersection of planes polyA and
    polyB"; it wraps Math3D_PlaneVsLineSegClosestPoint and the camera uses
    it.

  * D_80119D90 -> sWallFlags
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the in-tree comment above it reads
    "SurfaceType_GetWallFlags, SurfaceType wall types"; it maps each
    WALL_TYPE to the WALL_FLAG bits SurfaceType_GetWallFlags returns.

  * D_80119E10 -> sFootstepSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the in-tree comment above it reads
    "SurfaceType_GetSfx"; it maps a surface material index to the matching
    NA_SE_PL_WALK_* sound.

  * func_800425B0 -> WaterBox_GetSurface3
    deduced, confidence HIGH: its doc comment says it is the "same as
    WaterBox_GetSurfaceImpl, but tests if WaterBox properties & 0x80000 !=
    0". It joins the file's existing WaterBox_GetSurface1 /
    WaterBox_GetSurface2 / WaterBox_GetSurfaceImpl family as the third
    numbered variant; nothing calls it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
